print(best[1])
print(best[2])
```
wrong[[0, 0, 3, 3, 3, 3]\n[0, 0, 3, 4, 4, 4]]: With 5 kg, both items fit together: 3 + 4 = 7.
wrong[[0, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3]\n[0, 3, 3, 4, 7, 7]]: The 2 kg item doesn't fit a 1 kg bag: best[1][1] is 0.
wrong[[0, 0, 3, 3, 6, 6]\n[0, 0, 3, 4, 6, 7]]: Each item can be taken only once: with only the first item, the best is 3, however big the bag.
hint: Row 1: the 2 kg item, worth 3, fits from capacity 2 on.
hint: Row 2: take the 3 kg item when best[1][w − 3] + 4 beats best[1][w].
right: `[0, 0, 3, 3, 3, 3]` and `[0, 0, 3, 4, 4, 7]`. At capacity 5 both items fit: 7.
wrong: Row 1 is 3 from capacity 2. Row 2: capacity 3 takes the second item (4), capacity 4 too, and capacity 5 adds it to best[1][2] = 3: 7.

=== task: mcq easy
title: The row above
prompt: Taking item i − 1 in box (i, w) reads `best[i - 1][w - wt]`, from the row above. Why that row?
* It holds the best from the earlier items only, so the leftover room is filled without item i − 1, which is then used at most once :: Right. Reading the same row could put the item in again.
- Because the row above is always larger :: The row above can't be larger: each row can only add choices.
- Because the current row isn't filled yet :: Part of it is: the boxes to the left are filled. Using them would allow the item twice.
- To skip the item :: Skipping reads `best[i - 1][w]`; the take option reads the row above at w − wt.
hint: Which items are available in row i − 1?
hint: What would `best[i][w - wt]` already include?
right: The row above knows nothing of item i − 1, so adding its value once gives a set that uses it exactly once. That's the 0/1 rule.
wrong: The leftover room must be filled from earlier items only, which is what the row above holds.

